        <OtherAbstract Type="PIP" Language="eng">
            <AbstractText>The authors' previous research has demonstrated that treatment with 50-100 mg/day of mifepristone produces symptomatic improvement of endometriosis. The present study assessed the efficacy of a substantially reduced antiprogesterone dose. After a baseline cycle, 7 US women with pelvic pain caused by endometriosis were administered 5 mg of mifepristone daily for 6 months. Clinical responsiveness was evaluated in daily symptom inventories completed by study participants. Pelvic pain improved significantly within 1 month of treatment initiation in 6 of 7 patients. Cyclic bleeding ceased in all 7 patients, but 4 women complained of irregular bleeding that begin 3-5 months into treatment. Menstrual cyclicity returned 23-37 days after study completion. 2 women did not complete the study--1 because of continued pelvic pain and 1 because of heavy bleeding during the fifth cycle. Surgical staging through laparoscopy in the 5 remaining women failed to document a significant change in endometriosis. 1 woman had a slight increase in endometriosis score compared to baseline, 1 had no change, and the remaining 3 women showed decreases from 32-45%. Given the failure of the 5 mg dose to stabilize the endometrium and the high rate of uterine bleeding, a dose of 50 mg of mifepristone is recommended for future investigations.</AbstractText>
        </OtherAbstract>
